## Confidence distribution

Every article carries a dots rating for how much confidence a reader can place in its finding. The distribution below shows how much of the report rests on strong evidence and how much is still single-source.

| Rating | Meaning | Articles | Share |
|---|---|---|---|
| ●●●● Established | Peer-reviewed and independently confirmed, or a primary regulatory/government document | 12 | 14% |
| ●●●○ Corroborated | Peer-reviewed single study, or a credible primary source confirmed by independent reporting | 23 | 28% |
| ●●○○ Reported | Single credible source, not yet independently verified | 43 | 52% |
| ●○○○ Claimed | Vendor claim without disclosed methodology, or a single uncorroborated report | 5 | 6% |

**42% of articles are Established or Corroborated.** The rest are directional — worth knowing, not yet a basis for a major commitment. When a rating is in doubt, the lower level is assigned.

